/* 
  The Traditional CSRF Token Defense Pattern (for monolithic apps): 
*/

// 1. The server generates a random string (CSRF Token) and puts it in a hidden 
// field on the login form.
<form action="/transfer" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="8f3jd92k3jd">
  <button>Transfer Money</button>
</form>

// 2. When submitted, the server verifies the cookie AND verifies the token matches.
// Evilsite can force your browser to send the cookie, but Evilsite cannot read 
// the random token from your HTML, so their forged request will fail.
